      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the tip. i checked the trust relationship and it looks fine, and i'm pretty sure the bucket policy is open enough for now just to test.\n\nwait i just realized something looking at my policy again. i only put the bucket ARN in the resource field, i didn't put the path for the objects inside the bucket. would that be why it's failing? i might need to add '/*' to the end of the ARN, right? \n\nalso i noticed the error says \"Access Denied\" specifically when it tries to create the folder structure for the logs. does that change anything?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "By not including the path in the `Resource` field, the policy is only granting permission to upload objects to the root of the bucket, not inside any folders. Adding `/*` to the end of the ARN should resolve this. \n\nRegarding the \"Access Denied\" specifically for folder creation, this is a possible issue due to the fact that the policy allows `s3:PutObject` but not `s3:PutObject Receipt`, which is required for folders. You can add the `s3:PutObject Receipt` permission to your policy to address this.",
      "source": "target",
